  "enhanced_information": "The plasma membrane of a cell is primarily anchored to the cytoskeleton through the interaction of spectrin and ankyrin. Spectrin is a cytoskeletal protein that forms a meshwork beneath the plasma membrane, providing structural support and maintaining cell shape. Ankyrin serves as a linker protein that connects spectrin to integral membrane proteins, facilitating the attachment of the cytoskeleton to the plasma membrane. Tubulin, while a component of microtubules, does not directly anchor the plasma membrane to the cytoskeleton, making it less relevant in this context. Laminin, an extracellular matrix protein, does not play a direct role in linking the plasma membrane to the cytoskeleton. Therefore, the most accurate options are spectrin and ankyrin, with ankyrin being essential for the connection of spectrin to membrane proteins.",
